      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the smallest four-digit number divisible by \\(33\\), we need to determine the least positive four-digit integer that is divisible by \\(33\\). \n\nHere's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. Identify the smallest four-digit number:\n   The smallest four-digit number is \\(1000\\).\n\n2. Determine if it is divisible by \\(33\\):\n   \\[\n   1000 \\div 33 \\approx 30.3030 \\ldots\n   \\]\n   Since \\(1000\\) divided by \\(33\\) does not yield an integer, we cannot divide \\(1000\\) directly by \\(33\\). Therefore, we look for the next number greater than 1000 that is divisible by \\(33\\).\n\n3. Find factors of \\(33\\):\n   Factors of \\(33\\) are: \\(1, 3,\\) \\(11,\\) and \\(33\\).\n\n4. Check sequence by increasing multiples of \\(33\\):\n   Start with the smallest multiple greater than \\(1000\\) obtained by division by \\(33\\), i.e., \n   \\[\n   1000 + (33 - (1000 \\mod 33))\n   \\]\n\n5. Calculate the multiplication:\n   \\[\n   1000 + (33 - (1000 \\mod 33))\n   \\]\n\n6. Perform the division to ensure integer values:\n   Let’s perform the division:\n   \\[\n   1000 \\div 33 \\approx 30 \\ \\text{with a remainder of} 10\n   \\]\n   \n   Then add the divisor \\(33\\) yield:\n   \\[\n   1000 + (33 - 10) = 1000 + 23 = 1023\n   \\]\n\n7. Verify if \\(1023\\) is indeed divisible by \\(33\\):\n   \\[\n   1023 \\div 33 = 31\n   \\]\n\nThus, \\(1023\\) is divisible by \\(33\\). Therefore, the smallest four-digit number that is divisible by \\(33\\) is \\(\\boxed{1023}\\).",
